    "text": "# Appointment Reminder Service\n\n## Scope\n\nA small service that, given an appointment (patient, provider, start time, channel), delivers a reminder at a configured lead time (e.g., 24h and 1h before). Assume SMS/email via a provider API. Assume a Postgres database and a pool of workers.\n\n## Data model\n\n```\nappointments(id, patient_id, start_at_utc, tz, status, version)\nreminders(id, appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc, state, attempt,\n          next_attempt_at, lease_until, provider_msg_id, idem_key, sent_at)\n```\n\n- `fire_at_utc` is computed once at scheduling time from `start_at_utc` and the appointment's IANA `tz`. All storage is UTC; the tz is only used to compute the wall-clock time the user expects.\n- `idem_key` is deterministic: `sha256(appointment_id || channel || fire_at_utc || appointment.version)`. This is the key that makes retries safe.\n- `state \u2208 {pending, in_flight, sent, failed, cancelled}`.\n\n## Scheduling and time zones\n\n- Store `start_at_utc` and the IANA `tz` (e.g., `America/New_York`), never a fixed offset. Offsets change with DST.\n- Compute `fire_at_utc = start_at_utc - lead_time`. Do this in UTC; the tz only matters for display and for \"9am local\" style rules.\n- If the rule is \"9am local the day before,\" compute the local date, then resolve 9am in that tz via a tz library (handles DST gaps/overlaps). If the local time doesn't exist (spring-forward gap), pick",
